## Advantages of Compile-Time Checking

Reporting undefined global variables during compilation
would help programmers identify mistakes earlier.

It could also make debugging easier because errors
would be detected without requiring the affected
function to execute.

This would be particularly useful in larger programs
where some functions are rarely called.

## Disadvantages of Compile-Time Checking

Strict compile-time checking could make development
less flexible.

For example, a programmer might temporarily leave
unfinished code in a function while working on another
part of the program.

Even if that function is never called, the compiler
could reject the entire script.

This could make testing and refactoring less convenient.

## Comparison With Other Languages

### Python

Python generally resolves global names at runtime.

A function may reference a global variable that has
not been defined yet, as long as the variable exists
when the function actually executes.

If the name cannot be resolved when used, Python raises
a NameError.

### JavaScript

JavaScript also allows functions to reference variables
that may be declared elsewhere.

For example, a function may be defined before the
global variable it uses.

However, accessing an unresolvable identifier typically
produces a ReferenceError.

JavaScript also has additional rules involving
declaration types, hoisting, and the temporal dead zone.

## Alternative Approach: Compiler Warnings

Another option would be to report undeclared global
variables as warnings rather than errors.

This would allow programmers to run their scripts
while still being informed about potential mistakes.

However, warnings introduce additional decisions
about whether they should prevent execution.

Some developers may ignore warnings, while others
may want to treat every warning as an error.
